UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 31, Lesson 1: Balance and weight transfer', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Balance is controlling the body''s position over its base of support. A wide base (feet apart, more body parts touching the floor), a low centre of mass, tight tummy muscles and eyes fixed on a still spot all help balance. Weight transfer is moving body weight from one body part to another, such as from both feet to one foot. Use a mat or soft grass in a clear space, hold balances only as long as feels comfortable and stop if anything hurts.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same idea to a new game or situation, then compare two choices for safety and inclusion.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nTariq wobbles when he lifts one foot and looks at his feet. Write two cues to help him balance and say why they work.\n\nFEEDBACK\nCues: ''eyes on a still spot'' and ''tight tummy, arms out''. A still point helps the brain steady the body, and tight muscles and wide arms stop the body swaying.\n\nWATCH FOR\nThinking balance is only about strong legs. Balance uses the eyes, the tummy muscles and a good base of support.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nOn a mat or soft grass, practise four balances: two feet, one foot, a three-point balance (two feet and one hand) and a stork stand. Hold each for 5 seconds, record how long you held each and choose one cue to improve. Give an easier option for each, such as holding a wall.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one choice you changed and why. Any movement is optional, adult-supervised and can be done in a gentler way or described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"PDHPE","learning_intention":"Balance is controlling the body''s position over its base of support. A wide base (feet apart, more body parts touching the floor), a low centre of mass, tight tummy muscles and eyes fixed on a still spot all help balance. Weight transfer is moving body weight from one body part to another, such as from both feet to one foot. Use a mat or soft grass in a clear space, hold balances only as long as feels comfortable and stop if anything hurts.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Learn and discuss","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Clear, safe movement space with an adult nearby for any physical activity","Optional: soft ball, cushion or water bottle"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["PH2-MSP-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: On a mat or soft grass, practise four balances: two feet, one foot, a three-point balance (two feet and one hand) and a stork stand. Hold each for 5 seconds, record how long you held each and choose one cue to improve. G Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using balance on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: Thinking balance is only about strong legs.. Correction: Cues: ''eyes on a still spot'' and ''tight tummy, arms out''.. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Thinking balance is only about strong legs. Balance uses the eyes, the tummy muscles and a good base of support. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating balance on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with balance, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about balance with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for balance. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for balance. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A claim that names no evidence from the lesson material","A method that skips the key step taught in this lesson","Cues: ''eyes on a still spot'' and ''tight tummy, arms out''","Thinking balance is only about strong legs"],"question":"Tariq wobbles when he lifts one foot and looks at his feet. Write two cues to help him balance and say why they work. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for balance; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Balance is controlling the body''s position over its base of support","Cues: ''eyes on a still spot'' and ''tight tummy, arms out''","Careful use of base of support and explain why it matte with clear evidence","Thinking balance is only about strong legs"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with balance?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named base of support and explain why it matte, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Name base of support and explain why it matte only, without linking it to balance","Apply balance to new material and name the base of support and explain why it matte used","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence","Thinking balance is only about strong legs"],"question":"Which response best applies balance with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The stronger choice uses safe, healthy or respectful choices appropriately for this lesson on balance. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Cues: ''eyes on a still spot'' and ''tight tummy, arms out''","Choose the fastest option even when it ignores safety","Use a put-down and call it honesty","Thinking balance is only about strong legs"],"question":"Which response best uses safe, healthy or respectful choices for balance?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Choose the fastest option even when it ignores safety","Use a put-down and call it honesty","Thinking balance is only about strong legs","Name a body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy and say how it helps with balance"],"question":"Which evidence move best supports a claim about balance?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea (Balance is controlling the body''s position over its base of support.) with concrete detail. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Choose the fastest option even when it ignores safety","Use a put-down and call it honesty","Example: Rosa holds a one-foot balance for 5 seconds","Thinking balance is only about strong legs"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about balance?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Accuracy means checking evidence and honouring limits or protocols. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Check safety and fairness, include others, and explain the healthier choice","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster","Treat every claim as equally reliable without checking a source or cue"],"question":"Which action keeps safe, healthy or respectful choices accurate for balance?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the taught skill. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["On a mat or soft grass, practise four balances: two feet, one foot, a three-point balance (two feet and one hand) and a","Retype the worked example with no new example","Thinking balance is only about strong legs","Name base of support and explain why it matte only, with no linked example"],"question":"Which independent response best applies balance to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about balance with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating balance on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using balance on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for balance. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with balance, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Thinking balance is only about strong legs.. Correction: Cues: ''eyes on a still spot'' and ''tight tummy, arms out''.. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested. Share only what you feel comfortable sharing.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-pdhpe-y3-w31-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA PDHPE alignment using catalogue outcome codes (PH2-*); content-point mapping remains pending human review. Physical activities are optional, adult-supervised and adaptable; no child is required to share personal details.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise balance.","Read the mission before you start moving or deciding.","Keep your own safety and everyone''s inclusion in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not keep everyone safe and included."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the cue or rule that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore safety, consent or inclusion.","Prefer a reasoned, respectful choice over one that merely looks fun."],"submit":["Confirm that your reasons and reflection are both present.","Proofread names, steps and safety words.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact cue, rule or choice before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key cues.","Say what each cue does for safety, health or friendship.","A final label alone is weaker than a stated reason."],"teach2":["Compare an unsafe or unkind choice with a better one.","Change the choice or strategy, not only the words.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh place, game or situation that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original reasons and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill in a new situation."]},"subject":"Movement Skill and Performance","success_criteria":["I can use balance with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify base of support and explain why it matters.","I can practise weight transfer independently on a new prompt.","I can notice a still spot needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Balance is controlling the body''s position over its base of support.","options":["An answer copied without understanding","Balance is controlling the body''s position over its base of support","A guess with no reason","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about balance with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: Rosa holds a one-foot balance for 5 seconds. Cues: wide base first (feet apart), eyes on a spot on the wall, tight tummy, arms out. To make it harder she lifts one foot and keeps her arms wide. Weight transfer: rock from both feet to the right foot, hold, then back, slowly.\n\nTRY FIRST\nTariq wobbles when he lifts one foot and looks at his feet. Write two cues to help him balance and say why they work.\n\nFEEDBACK\nCues: ''eyes on a still spot'' and ''tight tummy, arms out''. A still point helps the brain steady the body, and tight muscles and wide arms stop the body swaying.","explain":"Balance is controlling the body''s position over its base of support. A wide base (feet apart, more body parts touching the floor), a low centre of mass, tight tummy muscles and eyes fixed on a still spot all help balance. Weight transfer is moving body weight from one body part to another, such as from both feet to one foot. Use a mat or soft grass in a clear space, hold balances only as long as feels comfortable and stop if anything hurts.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Thinking balance is only about strong legs. Balance uses the eyes, the tummy muscles and a good base of support. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"Wide and small bases.","caption":"A wider base steadies you.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Wide base</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">feet apart, still spot</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Small base</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">one foot, eyes down</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which cue or rule matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the cues, rules or choices that keep everyone safe and included before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this example: Example: Rosa holds a one-foot balance for 5 seconds. Cues: wide base first (feet apart), eyes on a spot on the wall, ti Then write your own reasons.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which cue or rule matters most.\nJuno: Name the cues, rules or choices that keep everyone safe and included before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this example: Example: Rosa holds a one-foot balance for 5 seconds. Cues: wide base first (feet apart), eyes on a spot on the wall, ti Then write your own reasons."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others.","options":["A guess with no reason","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect","Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about balance with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: Rosa holds a one-foot balance for 5 seconds. Cues: wide base first (feet apart), eyes on a spot on the wall, tight tummy, arms out. To make it harder she lifts one foot and keeps her arms wide. Weight transfer: rock from both feet to the right foot, hold, then back, slowly. Look at each cue, rule or choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key cues, rules or choices from the example and say what each one does for safety, health or relationships.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful reasoning names the cue, rule or strategy and the reason it helps, not only a label such as \"good\".","explain":"Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without a reason does not show PDHPE thinking. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Show the reasoning","visual":[{"alt":"Balance cues and holding time.","caption":"Hold still and breathe.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Cues</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">tight tummy, arms out</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Time</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">hold for 5 seconds</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison.","options":["A guess with no reason","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect","Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about balance with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nThinking balance is only about strong legs. Balance uses the eyes, the tummy muscles and a good base of support. Here is a more helpful example: Example: Rosa holds a one-foot balance for 5 seconds. Cues: wide base first (feet apart), eyes on a spot on the wall, tight tummy, arms out. To make it harder she lifts one foot and keeps her arms wide. Weight transfer: rock from both feet to the right foot, hold, then back, slowly.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against choice does not work, then write a safer, kinder or healthier choice with a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nCues: ''eyes on a still spot'' and ''tight tummy, arms out''. A still point helps the brain steady the body, and tight muscles and wide arms stop the body swaying.","explain":"Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison. Name the specific PDHPE error, then show the safer or more respectful choice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the wording without changing the choice does not fix the error. Thinking balance is only about strong legs. Balance uses the eyes, the tummy muscles and a good base of support. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example.","options":["Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no reason"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about balance with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nBalance is controlling the body''s position over its base of support. A wide base (feet apart, more body parts touching the floor), a low centre of mass, tight tummy muscles and eyes fixed on a still spot all help balance. Weight transfer is moving body weight from one body part to another, such as from both feet to one foot. Use a mat or soft grass in a clear space, hold balances only as long as feels comfortable and stop if anything hurts. Now choose a different situation to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe or practise one fresh situation that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new situation should use the same PDHPE idea as the example, but happen in a different place or with different people.","explain":"Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example. Review safety, fairness and inclusion.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new situation. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Transfer to a new situation"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 31, Lesson 1: Balance and weight transfer","unit_number":7,"unit_title":"Gymnastics and dance","week":31,"week_in_unit":1,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nExample: Rosa holds a one-foot balance for 5 seconds. Cues: wide base first (feet apart), eyes on a spot on the wall, tight tummy, arms out. To make it harder she lifts one foot and keeps her arms wide. Weight transfer: rock from both feet to the right foot, hold, then back, slowly.\n\nWrite what makes this example safe, healthy or respectful, naming the cues, rules or choices used.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Wide and small bases.","caption":"A wider base steadies you.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Wide base</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">feet apart, still spot</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Small base</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">one foot, eyes down</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Balance cues and holding time.","caption":"Hold still and breathe.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Cues</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">tight tummy, arms out</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Time</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">hold for 5 seconds</text></g></svg>"}],"year_level":"Year 3"}') WHERE seed_key='s2-pdhpe-y3-w31-l1' AND family_id IS NULL;
